---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';
const checker =
  import.meta.env.PUBLIC_CHECKER_ORIGIN || 'http://localhost:5173';
---

<Page
  article
  title="Prepare your Instagram followers export for local comparison"
  description="Use Meta’s official export instructions, then prepare JSON files for local comparison. Include both directions, every split part, and a known source date."
>
  <p class="eyebrow">GUIDE · OPTIONAL LOCAL IMPORT</p><h1>
    Prepare your Instagram export.
  </h1><p class="lede">
    If you already have an export, you can compare its supported JSON files
    locally in the MutualLens preview. An export is not the required automatic
    workflow.
  </p>
  <aside class="article-note">
    <strong>Verification note · September 7, 2026</strong><p>
      Meta’s public announcement confirms that Instagram information-download
      tools are in Accounts Center. The detailed Help Center page redirected to
      a login screen during review, so the current exact menu sequence and
      export-generation flow have not been verified live here. Follow Meta’s
      current instructions if its labels differ.
    </p>
  </aside>
  <h2>Use the official export controls</h2><p>
    Open <a
      href="https://www.facebook.com/help/instagram/181231772500920"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
      target="_blank">Instagram’s official information-export guide</a
    > and follow the instructions for your account. Meta describes the information-download
    tools in <a
      href="https://about.fb.com/news/2023/10/manage-your-information-across-apps/"
      rel="noopener noreferrer"
      target="_blank">its Accounts Center announcement</a
    >. Any account authentication belongs on Meta’s own website or app.
  </p>
  <h2>Prepare files the checker can read</h2><ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Use the account you own or manage.</strong> Confirm the selected profile
      before preparing its information.
    </li><li>
      <strong>Choose JSON for this checker.</strong> MutualLens’s tested parser supports
      JSON relationships data, not HTML pages. Where export controls offer information
      categories, choose followers and following to avoid unnecessary unrelated files.
    </li><li>
      <strong>Include both directions and all parts.</strong> Supported names include
      <code>followers_1.json</code>, additional numbered follower parts, and <code
        >following.json</code
      >. Keep split files together. Select an all-time date range where the
      official controls offer one; a limited range may be unsuitable for
      establishing absence.
    </li><li>
      <strong>Keep the source date if known.</strong> Do not substitute the date you
      opened or imported the ZIP. Leave an unknown collection date blank.
    </li><li>
      <strong>Select the ZIP or all relevant loose JSON files.</strong> The checker
      asks for the account identity and an explicit all-parts confirmation. It processes
      files on your device.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Confirmation has a specific meaning</h2><p>
    The all-parts checkbox says that you selected every part you received for
    both directions. It does not independently prove that the platform export is
    complete, current, or free from omissions. Results are labeled complete for
    the supplied source only when the import’s completeness rules are met.
  </p>
  <h2>If the export does not work</h2><p>
    Keep the original files. Read the checker’s specific error before changing
    anything. Do not rename unrelated JSON to a follower filename or remove
    malformed records to force a report. A fresh supported export may be needed
    when formats change.
  </p><p>
    <a href="/guides/instagram-export-troubleshooting/"
      >Troubleshoot missing or unsupported files</a
    >, or <a href={`${checker}/#import`}>open the local import preview</a>.
  </p>
  <p class="small muted">
    This is preparation guidance for a tested local parser, not evidence of a
    successful live Instagram export or automatic account scan.
  </p>
</Page>
